    "slug": "angular-signals-guide",
    "excerpt": "Signals are now the heart of reactivity in Angular. Here is a practical, example-first guide to signal(), computed(), effect(), signal inputs and how they sit alongside RxJS.",
    "tag": "DEEP DIVE",
    "category": "Angular",
    "publishedAt": "2026-05-31T10:00:00Z",
    "readMins": 8,
    "coverUrl": null,
    "coverAlt": "Angular Signals — The 2026 Guide to Reactive State",
    "bodyHtml": "<p>Signals are the biggest shift in Angular since standalone components. They give Angular a <code>fine-grained</code> reactivity model: the framework knows exactly which values a template uses, so it can update just those parts of the DOM — no more zone-wide change detection guesswork.</p><h2>What is a signal?</h2><p>A signal is a wrapper around a value that notifies anything depending on it when the value changes. You read it by calling it like a function (<code>count()</code>) and you write it with <code>set()</code> or <code>update()</code>.</p><pre data-label=\"count.ts\"><code class=\"language-typescript\">import { signal } from '@angular/core';\n\nconst count = signal(0);\n\ncount();            // read  → 0\ncount.set(5);       // write → 5\ncount.update(n =&gt; n + 1); // 6</code></pre><h2>computed() — derived state</h2><p>A <code>computed()</code> signal derives its value from other signals and recomputes automatically — and only when something it actually reads has changed. It is also memoised.</p><pre data-label=\"total.ts\"><code class=\"language-typescript\">import { signal, computed } from '@angular/core';\n\nconst price = signal(100);\nconst qty = signal(2);\n\nconst total = computed(() =&gt; price() * qty()); // 200\nqty.set(3);          // total re-computes lazily → 300</code></pre><h2>effect() — running side effects</h2><p>Use <code>effect()</code> for side effects that should re-run when their signals change: logging, syncing to localStorage, integrating non-Angular libraries. Do not use it to set other signals — use <code>computed()</code> for derived state.</p><pre data-label=\"typescript\"><code class=\"language-typescript\">import { effect } from '@angular/core';\n\neffect(() =&gt; {\n  // Re-runs whenever theme() changes\n  localStorage.setItem('theme', theme());\n});</code></pre><h2>Signal inputs and model()</h2><p>Modern Angular replaces the <code>@Input()</code> decorator with signal-based inputs, and adds two-way <code>model()</code> signals:</p><pre data-label=\"counter.component.ts\"><code class=\"language-typescript\">import { Component, input, model } from '@angular/core';\n\n@Component({ selector: 'app-counter', /* ... */ })\nexport class CounterComponent {\n  // &lt;app-counter [start]=\"5\" /&gt;\n  start = input(0);            // readonly signal input\n  label = input.required&lt;string&gt;();\n\n  // two-way: &lt;app-counter [(value)]=\"x\" /&gt;\n  value = model(0);\n\n  increment() { this.value.update(v =&gt; v + 1); }\n}</code></pre><h2>Signals vs RxJS — when to use which</h2><p>They are partners, not rivals. The rule of thumb seniors use:</p><ul><li><code>signal()</code> for synchronous UI state that always has a current value.</li><li>RxJS for streams of events over time — debounced search, websockets, retries.</li><li><code>toSignal()</code> to bridge an Observable into a signal at the edge of your component.</li></ul><pre data-label=\"typescript\"><code class=\"language-typescript\">import { toSignal } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';\n\nresults = toSignal(\n  this.search.valueChanges.pipe(debounceTime(300), switchMap(q =&gt; this.api.find(q))),\n  { initialValue: [] }\n);</code></pre><h2>A full component</h2><pre data-label=\"cart.component.ts\"><code class=\"language-typescript\">import { Component, signal, computed } from '@angular/core';\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-cart',\n  template: `\n    &lt;button (click)=\"add()\"&gt;Add item&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Items: {{ count() }} · Total: {{ total() }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  `,\n})\nexport class CartComponent {\n  count = signal(0);\n  price = signal(199);\n  total = computed(() =&gt; this.count() * this.price());\n\n  add() { this.count.update(n =&gt; n + 1); }\n}</code></pre><h2>Takeaways</h2><p>Reach for <code>signal()</code> for state, <code>computed()</code> for anything derived, <code>effect()</code> for side effects, and <code>toSignal()</code> to bring RxJS streams in. Master these four and you have modern Angular reactivity covered.</p>",
